[09:16:13] user
so the real product value isnt answering questions. its keeping students in the learning context

[09:16:19] assistant
Exactly. The metric that matters isn't 'questions answered' — it's 'minutes of uninterrupted
          learning preserved.' The assistant is a context anchor. The answer is secondary to not
          breaking the student's current mental model.

[09:16:59] user
ok but research on desirable difficulty says struggle improves retention. if we just give answers
          immediately are we actually hurting learning outcomes

[09:17:09] assistant
Yes — this is Robert Bjork's work. Immediate correct answers reduce long-term retention compared to
          effortful retrieval, even failed retrieval. The nuance: it's question-type dependent.
          Factual lookup ('what is the syntax for X') benefits from immediate answers. Conceptual
          understanding ('why does X work this way') benefits from guided discovery. Your assistant
          needs to distinguish these and respond differently to each.

[09:17:34] user
so two modes. direct answer for factual, socratic for conceptual. how do we classify which is which
          without overcomplicating it

[09:17:45] assistant
Query classification at input. Conceptual signals: 'why', 'how does', 'explain', comparisons, cause-
          effect questions. Factual signals: 'what is', 'show me', 'syntax for', 'how do I'.
          Classify with a keyword heuristic for v1 — get it right 80% of the time, 20%
          misclassification is acceptable, students can prompt for a different mode. Log
          misclassification complaints — that's your training data for a better classifier.

[09:18:20] user
