[09:20:47] user
before we go to architecture — is this even a RAG problem or should we fine-tune a model on our
          content

[09:21:01] assistant
Different tools. Fine-tuning bakes knowledge into weights — good for tone and style, bad for factual
          recall: fine-tuned models still hallucinate, and when course content updates you retrain.
          RAG retrieves from a live index — knowledge is always current, sources are verifiable, new
          content is live without touching the model. For a learning platform where curriculum
          evolves, RAG is right. Fine-tuning on top of RAG for pedagogical style is the long-term
          play, not the starting point.


──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  02 — Architecture Decisions
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

[09:21:31] user
embedding models. ada-002 vs 3-small vs 3-large vs open source like e5 or bge. give me real
          tradeoffs not marketing copy

[09:21:44] assistant
ada-002: 1536 dims, solid baseline, being deprecated. text-embedding-3-small: better MTEB scores,
          $0.00002/1K tokens, supports Matryoshka dimension reduction. text-embedding-3-large: 3072
          dims, highest quality, 5x the storage. E5-large/BGE-M3: open source, self-hosted, no per-
          token cost, competitive with 3-small on retrieval benchmarks. Under 2M chunks: 3-small is
          pragmatic. Above that, BGE-M3 on your own infra starts making economic sense.

[09:22:12] user
matryoshka embeddings — is that actually useful or just a cool name

[09:22:20] assistant
It's real. OpenAI reports 1–2% accuracy drop truncating 1536-dim vectors to 256 dims. At 512 dims
          you're using 67% less storage and HNSW queries are meaningfully faster. Worth using if
          storage cost is a constraint — use 512-dim truncated vectors and fall back to full 1536
          only if you measure retrieval degradation in eval.

[09:22:55] user
vector store. pgvector vs dedicated. at wat scale does pgvector actually become a problem

[09:23:06] assistant
pgvector HNSW starts showing latency degradation around 5–10M vectors at high concurrency (50+ QPS).
          Below that, with tuned index parameters, you stay under 20ms p95. The earlier bottleneck:
          write performance during bulk ingestion — HNSW doesn't support concurrent writes well. For
          a learning platform adding content periodically, manageable. Review checkpoint at 1M
          vectors — benchmark then, decide if Qdrant or Weaviate buys you anything real.

[09:23:26] user
small-to-big retrieval — explain with one concrete example

[09:23:35] assistant
Student asks 'what is a deadlock?' Your index has 128-token chunks. Retrieved chunk: 'A deadlock
          occurs when two processes each hold a resource the other needs.' Correct but thin. Small-
          to-big: each small chunk has a parent_chunk_id pointing to its 512-token parent. When you
          retrieve the small chunk, you pass the parent to the LLM. Same retrieval precision, the
          LLM now has the definition plus surrounding explanation of why it matters. One column in
          the schema enables this entirely.

[09:24:01] user
hybrid search BM25 plus vector — how do we actually combine the scores. theyre on different scales

[09:24:09] assistant
Reciprocal Rank Fusion. Rank each result list independently, combine ranks via 1/(k + rank). No
          score normalization needed — handles the scale mismatch automatically. Linear combination
          requires calibrating alpha and normalizing scores. Learned fusion is best quality but most
          complex. RRF matches learned fusion within a few percentage points on most retrieval
          benchmarks and requires zero calibration. Start here.

[09:24:39] user
reranking — cross encoders. wen does it help and wat does it cost

[09:24:49] assistant
Retrieve top-50 with vector search, rerank to top-5 with cross-encoder. Gain: 5–15% improvement in
          precision. Cost: 50–200ms added latency. Worth it when your corpus has many similar-
          looking chunks where bi-encoder similarity can't distinguish relevance. For structured
          curriculum content — less ambiguous by nature — the gain is smaller. Measure retrieval
          precision first. Add reranking only when you identify it as the specific failure mode.

[09:25:11] user
HyDE — what is it and should we use it here

[09:25:20] assistant
Hypothetical Document Embeddings. Instead of embedding the raw query, generate a hypothetical answer
          first, then embed that. The hypothetical answer is in the same semantic space as your
          document chunks, so retrieval is more accurate than embedding a terse question. Measured
          gains: 10–30% on retrieval benchmarks for short or ambiguous queries. Cost: one additional
          small-model call per query, 50–100ms. Worth it for conceptual queries. Skip for factual
          lookups where the query is already precise.

[09:25:23] judge:arch
          checking : architecture stack — embedding, chunking, retrieval, hybrid search
          verdict  : PASS
          notes    : Small-to-big correctly identified as high-value low-complexity for v1. RRF
                     for hybrid fusion is the right pragmatic call. Reranking correctly deferred.
                     HyDE included. One gap: no discussion of query-side spelling correction.
                     Student queries on mobile are often misspelled — 'asnyc' vs 'async' will miss
                     BM25 retrieval entirely. Add lightweight spell-correct pass on raw query
                     before retrieval.

[09:25:53] user
context window management. 5 chunks plus system prompt plus conversation history — how do we budget
          this

[09:26:03] assistant
Treat the window as a fixed resource with explicit allocation. For GPT-4o-mini (128K): 2K system
          prompt, 3K conversation history (last 4 turns), 4K retrieved context (5 chunks), 1K
          generation budget. The discipline: never let retrieved context crowd out conversation
          history. History carries the student's current understanding — it's what makes the tutor
          feel continuous. Chunks are replaceable each turn. History is not.


──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  03 — Security & Data
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

[09:26:41] user
security. multi tenant — different courses, students, instructors. wat are the actual threat
          vectors. dont skip any

[09:26:55] assistant
Four surfaces. One: retrieval boundary violation — student in course A retrieves course B content
          they're not enrolled in. Two: prompt injection — crafted message overrides system context
          or extracts other session data. Three: embedding inversion — if the vector store is
          compromised, inversion attacks can approximately reconstruct source text from vectors
          (Morris et al. 2023, 60–80% word accuracy on short chunks). Four: generation abuse —
          jailbreaking via educational framing.

[09:27:15] user
retrieval boundary — enforce this at the data layer not the API layer

[09:27:24] assistant
Every chunk gets metadata: course_id, access_tier. Every query is filtered at the vector search
          level — WHERE clause applied in pgvector before ANN search, not after. This runs at the
          database level, not application code. Application-level post-retrieval filtering can be
          bypassed by bugs or race conditions. Database-level filtering is a hard boundary — the
          wrong chunks never appear in the result set at all. Never rely on the LLM to decide what
          to include from retrieved context.

[09:27:49] user
prompt injection — its largely unsolved right. wat can we actually do practically

[09:28:00] assistant
Mitigatable in layers. One: input validation — detect and sanitize known injection patterns. Catches
          naive attempts, not sophisticated ones. Two: system prompt hardening — never include
          sensitive data in the system prompt; if extracted, attacker finds nothing useful. Three:
          output filtering — check whether the response contains content outside the student's
          enrolled curriculum before returning. Four: per-session rate limiting — limits systematic
          extraction even if injection partially succeeds.

[09:28:22] user
embedding inversion — i hadnt thought about this. how real is this for us specifically

[09:28:32] assistant
More real than most teams realize. For short chunks under 100 tokens, inversion accuracy is highest
          — precisely what we're storing. For a platform with proprietary or licensed course
          content, this is an IP risk if the vector store is compromised. Mitigations: store
          embeddings in a separate access-controlled store from raw chunks. Encrypt at rest. Never
          embed content you'd be uncomfortable having partially reconstructed — student PII,
          instructor private notes, licensed verbatim text.

[09:28:35] judge:arch
          checking : security model completeness for multi-tenant RAG
          verdict  : FLAG
          notes    : Embedding inversion correctly flagged — rare to see this raised proactively.
                     Two gaps: (1) No rate limiting per user to prevent systematic extraction via
                     many small semantically varied queries. (2) FERPA compliance — if US-based
                     students are involved and queries are logged with user IDs, those logs may
                     constitute education records requiring specific access controls and deletion
                     rights. Neither is a blocker but both must be addressed before production
                     student data enters the system.

[09:28:55] user
FERPA — are we actually in scope for this

[09:29:03] assistant
If you have US-based students and store any PII linked to education records, yes. FERPA covers quiz
          scores, course enrollment, learning history. Key question: does the RAG system log queries
          associated with user IDs? If yes, those logs are potentially education records.
          Practically: hash or pseudonymize user ID in query logs, or route analytics through a
          separate anonymized pipeline that's FERPA-safe by design.

[09:29:33] user
data retention. embeddings, conversation history, query logs. wat are our defaults

[09:29:42] assistant
Separate policies by data type. Embeddings: permanent until model version change forces reindex.
          Source chunks: tied to course lifecycle, delete when course is retired. Conversation
          history: session-scoped by default, opt-in for cross-session memory. Query logs: 90 days
          raw, then aggregate and anonymize. User-facing: students can delete their conversation
          history on demand. Required under GDPR for EU users and increasingly expected under CCPA.

[09:30:10] user
if a student uploads their own notes — who owns that and can it leak to other students

[09:30:20] assistant
Student-uploaded content goes into a per-user private index, never merged with shared curriculum
          index. Retrieval searches the student's personal index AND shared curriculum, never
          another student's personal index. Ownership: student retains it, platform gets a license
          for retrieval within that student's sessions only. This needs to be in Terms of Service,
          not just the architecture. A technical boundary without a legal boundary still creates
          liability.

[09:30:52] user
cache — if two students ask the same question, could one student's session data leak through a
          shared cache entry

[09:31:01] assistant
Cache key structure: course_id + content_version + query_hash — no user_id. Two students asking the
          same curriculum question hit the same cache entry, which is fine — the answer doesn't
          change per student. What must not share cache keys: anything including conversation
          history (user-specific) or anything from a student's personal document index. Separate
          cache namespaces for these. Shared cache is for curriculum Q&A only.


