  04 — User Behavior & Edge Cases
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

[09:31:03] context:USER_BEHAVIOR.md
Session: 20–40 min active. Enters mid-course. Questions cluster around current topic. 2–3 follow-ups
          per session before moving on. Uses assistant to avoid watching lectures. 34% mobile
          sessions. 18% re-entry rate after 48h. Most common drop: after a failed or unhelpful
          answer. Users rarely rate responses explicitly.

[09:31:28] user
18% re-entry after 48 hours is low. if they come back 3 days later their context is gone. wat does
          that experience look like

[09:31:38] assistant
Two continuity levels. Short-term (same day): conversation persists in browser state, restore
          without a server round-trip. Medium-term (within 7 days): persist last N turns server-
          side, 'resume your session' on return. Beyond 7 days: don't restore a stale conversation —
          confusing and probably irrelevant. Instead show a topic summary: 'Last time you were
          studying async programming and asked about deadlocks. Pick up there?' Uses stored
          metadata, not raw conversation history.

[09:32:00] user
34% mobile. does that change the architecture or just the UI

[09:32:08] assistant
Both. Mobile users have a 3-second abandon threshold vs 5–7 for desktop. Streaming responses are
          non-negotiable on mobile — first token under 1 second. Also: mobile users ask shorter,
          more fragmented queries. Your query preprocessing (spelling correction, HyDE for
          conceptual) matters more for this cohort than for desktop.

[09:32:43] user
student who keeps asking the same thing differently because they genuinely dont understand. thats a
          signal not noise

[09:32:54] assistant
Most important signal in the system. Keep a running list of question embeddings per session (max
          20). Cosine similarity check on each new message — similarity > 0.85 to any prior question
          triggers approach switch. Don't rephrase the same explanation, that never works. Change
          modality: if you explained with text, switch to a step-by-step example. The system should
          detect the stuck pattern and adapt, not repeat itself.

[09:33:22] user
how do we detect semantic similarity per session without a full embedding comparison on every
          message

[09:33:31] assistant
One embedding per message (already done for retrieval), then a fast dot product against a small in-
          session list (max 20 vectors). Total added compute: under 5ms per turn. Session-local
          state, no database write, evict after session ends. If you don't instrument this from day
          one you'll have no idea how many students are silently stuck on the same concept — it's
          invisible without this signal.

[09:34:01] user
student uploads a textbook PDF they dont have rights to. we cant police copyright but we have
          liability exposure

[09:34:11] assistant
Technical mitigations reduce but don't eliminate exposure. File size limit discourages bulk uploads.
          Rate limit on user uploads. Most importantly: user-uploaded content stays in per-user
          private index — never redistributed. Copyright liability stays with the user if content
          isn't redistributed from the platform. Clear Terms of Service stating user responsibility.
          Maintain the platform-as-conduit distinction architecturally.

[09:34:49] user
wat if the model generates something factually wrong about a topic a student is studying for an exam

[09:34:59] assistant
Two-layer response. Technical: citations alongside every answer make the source verifiable — student
          can check the underlying chunk. Faithfulness failures are detectable and loggable. Policy:
          clear disclaimer in UI that the assistant is a study aid, not a substitute for course
          materials or instructor judgment. The technical citation layer is the stronger protection
          — transparent sourcing is harder to argue against than a buried TOS disclaimer.


