  05 — Implementation
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

[09:35:44] user
ok lets actually build. data model first. all tables all columns no shortcuts

[09:35:58] assistant
Six tables.
courses       : id, title, track_id, access_tier, created_at, archived_at
documents     : id, course_id, title, source_type, raw_content_url, content_hash, status, created_at
chunks        : id, document_id, parent_chunk_id (nullable), text, token_count, position_index,
          metadata jsonb
embeddings    : id, chunk_id, vector vector(512), model_id, created_at
conversations : id, user_id, course_id, topic_summary, started_at, last_active_at
messages      : id, conversation_id, role, content, retrieved_chunk_ids int[], faithfulness_score
          float,
query_type (factual/conceptual), created_at

+ user_documents / user_chunks / user_embeddings — same schema, scoped to user_id instead of
          course_id

[09:36:13] user
why content_hash on documents

[09:36:19] assistant
Deduplication. Instructor uploads the same PDF twice — or edits and reuploads — you don't re-embed
          blindly. Hash the content on ingest. Hash matches existing document in same course: skip
          processing entirely. Partial match (same file, different version): diff at chunk level,
          re-embed only changed chunks. Reindexing a 200-page textbook is expensive. Hash-based
          dedup is a one-line check that saves significant compute over time.

[09:36:59] user
ingestion pipeline. full design — not pseudocode, actual flow

[09:37:12] assistant
Async queue-based. Upload endpoint: validate (MIME type, file size, user permissions) -> store raw
          file in object storage -> enqueue job with {document_id, storage_path, course_id}. Return
          immediately with job_id. Worker pipeline: fetch file -> extract text (pdf-parse for PDF,
          Whisper for video, passthrough for markdown) -> validate extraction (empty? image-only?
          minimum length?) -> chunk with overlap -> batch embed (100 chunks per API call) -> write
          chunks and embeddings -> mark document indexed. On failure: dead letter queue -> alert ->
          document.status = 'failed' with error_reason. Never block the upload response on
          processing.

[09:37:32] user
pdf-parse fails on scanned documents. image-only PDFs. wat then

[09:37:41] assistant
Detect early. After extraction: text length under 100 characters for a document over 1 page =
          probably scanned. For v1: fail fast with a clear user message — 'This PDF appears to be
          scanned. Please upload a text-based PDF or paste your content directly.' Don't silently
          produce near-empty extraction. Downstream hallucinations from bad context are worse than a
          clear error. OCR is v2.

[09:38:19] user
the query handler. end to end. wat happens between student sends message and first token arrives

[09:38:34] assistant
Step 1 : classify query type (factual/conceptual) — keyword heuristic, <10ms
Step 2 : for conceptual, run HyDE (generate hypothetical answer, embed that)
for factual, embed query directly
Step 3 : hybrid search — pgvector ANN filtered by enrolled course_ids + BM25 via pg_trgm, RRF fusion
Step 4 : small-to-big expansion — fetch parent chunks for all retrieved small chunks
Step 5 : deduplicate (same parent appearing via multiple children -> include once)
Step 6 : check session state — repeated concept? flag for approach switch
Step 7 : assemble context with chunks + last 4 conversation turns
Step 8 : stream generation via SSE
Step 9 : async faithfulness check on completed response — log everything

[09:38:56] user
step 9 is async — so we show the response before we know if its faithful. wat if it fails after the
          student already read it

[09:39:06] assistant
Show immediately, append caveat if faithfulness fails: 'I want to flag I'm not confident this is
          fully grounded in your course material — please verify.' Better UX for an educational
          assistant where the harm floor is low. Hard exception: if query touches a flagged category
          (medical, legal, financial) — don't generate at all for those, regardless of latency cost.

[09:39:34] user
API design. REST vs streaming vs both. wat does the SSE event structure look like

[09:39:46] assistant
Both. REST for management: POST /conversations, GET /conversations/:id/messages, DELETE
          /conversations/:id,
POST /documents. SSE for query responses at POST /conversations/:id/query.

SSE event sequence:
event: chunks_found  data: {chunk_ids, sources}     // UI shows 'Searching your async chapter...'
event: token         data: {text}                    // per token in stream
event: done          data: {faithfulness_score, citations: [{chunk_id, text, source_title}]}

[09:40:18] user
conversation memory. full history vs summarization

[09:40:27] assistant
Progressive summarization. Turns 1–4: verbatim in context. Turns 5–12: summarize older turns with a
          fast model — 'Student learned about event loops, asked about deadlocks. Misconception:
          confused OS-level and application-level locking.' Turn 12+: keep summary + last 4
          verbatim. This preserves what matters pedagogically without burning context budget on old
          message text. Store summary in conversations.topic_summary.

[09:40:52] user
citations in the UI — how do we surface them without cluttering the reading experience

[09:41:00] assistant
Inline footnote numbers, expandable on click. [1][2] superscripts in response text — don't interrupt
          reading flow. Collapsed 'Sources' section below expands to show chunk text + document
          title + page/timestamp. On mobile: tap superscript for a bottom sheet. Citations must be
          verifiable, not decorative. If a student can tap [1] and see the exact sentence from their
          lecture notes that grounded the answer, the assistant earns trust.


──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  06 — Testing & Evaluation
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

[09:41:02] context:TESTING.md
Framework: functional correctness, boundary behavior, failure modes, regression surface. AI outputs
          need probabilistic acceptance — thresholds not exact matches. Judge agents must be
          stateless, deterministic given same inputs, run in under 2s. Coverage targets: retrieval
          90%, generation 80%, integration 70%. All evals runnable on every PR.

[09:41:33] user
testing the retrieval layer. how do we know if retreival is actually good vs just feels good

[09:41:44] assistant
Golden retrieval set: 100–200 question-chunk pairs where you know the correct chunks for each
          question. Metrics: precision@5 (how many top-5 chunks are relevant), recall@5 (of all
          relevant chunks, how many in top 5), MRR (where does first relevant chunk appear). Run on
          every schema or config change. Baseline + regression threshold: precision@5 drops more
          than 5% = block the merge. Suite runs in minutes, automate it.

[09:42:06] user
how do we build those question-chunk pairs without spending weeks

[09:42:15] assistant
Synthetic generation. For each chunk: 'Generate 3 specific questions a student might ask where this
          chunk would be the ideal answer.' Store (question, chunk_id) pairs. Human-review 20% to
          remove bad pairs. 500 pairs in an afternoon. Synthetic bias risk: LLM generates questions
          in its own phrasing, not how students actually ask. Mitigate by replacing 30% of synthetic
          pairs with real user queries after 2 weeks in production.

[09:42:43] user
testing the generation layer. acceptance criteria. a correct answer isnt a string match

[09:42:54] assistant
LLM-as-judge with structured rubric. Four axes, 1–3 scale each: (1) Faithfulness — answer stays
          within retrieved context. (2) Correctness — factually accurate per the chunks. (3)
          Pedagogical quality — helps the student understand, not just informs. (4) Scope —
          addresses what was asked without over-explaining. Pass threshold: average >= 2.5, no
          single axis below 1.5. Run nightly against 50 fixed query-response pairs.

[09:43:18] user
the judge itself could be wrong or biased. how do we validate the judge

[09:43:28] assistant
Meta-evaluation. Build 30–40 responses with high-confidence human labels: clear passes and clear
          failures. Run the judge against these, measure judge accuracy — below 85% means the judge
          is unreliable, fix it before trusting it. Also: run multiple judge calls on the same
          response at temperature > 0, measure variance. High variance on the same response = judge
          is uncertain, lower confidence in that evaluation. Audit 10% of judge outputs weekly.

[09:43:58] user
regression testing. a prompt change breaks something that was working. how do we catch that before
          it ships

[09:44:08] assistant
Shadow eval on every PR touching retrieval config, prompt, or embedding model. Run full eval suite
          against main and the branch, diff the results. Block merge if any metric regresses beyond
          threshold. The discipline: never merge prompt changes without running eval. 'It's just a
          minor wording change' is how regressions ship. Minor prompt changes cause major behavior
          shifts — the eval suite is the only way to know which direction.

[09:44:30] user
query classifier testing. factual vs conceptual. we need to test that specifically

[09:44:38] assistant
50-query classification test set, manually labeled. Run against the classifier on every change
          touching classification logic. Measure precision and recall separately per class — a
          classifier that's 95% accurate by always predicting 'factual' is useless if 40% of real
          queries are conceptual. Log misclassification complaints from users explicitly — these
          become training data for a better classifier.

[09:45:10] user
load testing. how many concurrent users and how do we measure it

[09:45:21] assistant
Test two bottlenecks separately. Retrieval: pgvector ANN is CPU-bound — measure p50/p95/p99 at 10,
          50, 100 concurrent queries. Generation: test SSE connection stability under concurrent
          streaming load — 100 users streaming simultaneously stresses your event loop, not just the
          LLM API. Tool: k6 with realistic query distribution. Target: p95 first-token latency under
          2s at 50 concurrent users.

[09:45:46] user
load test with realistic queries — how do we get those before we have real users

[09:45:55] assistant
Generate from course content. For each major concept in curriculum, create 5 plausible student
          questions at varying complexity — factual and conceptual, well-formed and typo-ridden
          (representing mobile users). 10 concepts × 5 questions = 50 queries. Use the same
          synthetic set built for retrieval eval. The point: stress-test with queries that exercise
          the full pipeline, not synthetic load that bypasses the interesting parts.

[09:46:23] user
wat do we do wen the LLM API is down

[09:46:33] assistant
Graceful degradation in layers. Layer 1: retry with exponential backoff, 2 attempts at 1s and 3s.
          Layer 2: check response cache — if same query answered recently with high faithfulness,
          return cached. Layer 3: return retrieval-only — raw chunk text with no generation, labeled
          clearly. Layer 4: retrieval also degraded — clear error with expected resolution time.
          Each layer gives the user something rather than a broken experience.

[09:46:36] judge:arch
          checking : testing strategy completeness
          verdict  : PASS
          notes    : Meta-evaluation of the judge is a non-obvious inclusion — correct and
                     important. Shadow eval on PRs is the right gate. Query classifier test set
                     added correctly. One remaining gap: no E2E integration test covering the full
                     path from student query to citation in the UI. Unit tests on individual
                     components won't catch pipeline integration failures. Add one E2E test per
                     critical user journey.


──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  07 — Performance & Optimizations
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

[09:47:13] user
latency. target is first token under 1 second. where does time actually go in our pipeline

[09:47:27] assistant
Typical breakdown:
query classification            20–50ms
query embedding (API call)      80–150ms
pgvector ANN search              5–20ms
BM25 search                     10–30ms
RRF fusion                        ~1ms
small-to-big parent fetch        5–10ms
context assembly                  ~1ms
LLM first token                300–800ms
─────────────────────────────────────────
total to first token           420–1060ms

Two dominant costs: embedding API call and LLM first token. Priority: parallelize embedding
and BM25 (independent operations), cache frequent query embeddings, use a faster model for
first-token — GPT-4o-mini and Claude Haiku are 2–3x faster than full-size models.

[09:47:49] user
parallelize embedding and BM25 — show me how that works in the handler

[09:47:57] assistant
They're independent operations. Node: Promise.all([embedQuery(text), bm25Search(text, courseIds)]).
          Python async: asyncio.gather(embed_query(text), bm25_search(text, course_ids)). Embedding
          result feeds into ANN search, BM25 only needs raw text — both land at roughly the same
          time, then RRF fusion on combined lists. This alone saves 80–150ms — the embedding call no
          longer blocks BM25. For a sub-1-second target that's material.

[09:48:27] user
caching. wat exactly do we cache and at wat levels

[09:48:37] assistant
Three layers. One: query embedding cache — hash query string, store embedding, 24h TTL in Redis.
          Two: retrieval results cache — (course_id + content_version + query_hash -> chunk_ids), 1h
          TTL, invalidate on content update via version counter. Three: response cache for common
          questions — 'what is a deadlock' asked by 500 students hits cache after the first answer.
          Layer 3 is highest value at scale — common curriculum questions have very high repeat
          rates. Instrument cache hit rates from day one.

[09:48:59] user
content_version for cache invalidation — how do we track it without a distributed lock problem

[09:49:07] assistant
Per-course version counter, no locking needed. Postgres: UPDATE courses SET content_version =
          content_version + 1 WHERE id = $1 RETURNING content_version. Atomic increment, no race
          condition possible. Store current version in Redis for fast reads. When content updates:
          increment Postgres first, write new version to Redis. Old cache entries become unreachable
          (wrong version in key) and expire via TTL. Simple, no invalidation bugs.

[09:49:35] user
context compression — 5 chunks at 800 tokens. can we compress before sending to the LLM

[09:49:45] assistant
LLMLingua class of techniques. Token-level classifier predicts which tokens the LLM needs, removes
          low-probability ones. LLMLingua-2 (Microsoft, open source) achieves 4x compression with
          under 5% quality drop on most benchmarks. Risk: classifier makes mistakes on domain-
          specific technical vocabulary. Use with caution in v1 — add in v2 once you have baseline
          quality metrics to compare against.

[09:50:13] user
model routing — cheaper model for simple questions. how do we decide which query gets which model

[09:50:22] assistant
Complexity signals: factual + short + single concept -> Haiku/GPT-4o-mini. Conceptual + multi-part +
          synthesis -> Sonnet/GPT-4o. Upgrade triggers: 'why', 'explain', 'compare', query length >
          20 words, follow-up to prior conceptual question. The router should be a fast heuristic —
          not another LLM call. Keyword rules + query length + classification label from step 1. Log
          which model handled which query alongside faithfulness scores — tells you if thresholds
          need adjustment.

[09:50:25] judge:arch
          checking : cost optimization strategy
          verdict  : CONDITIONAL PASS
          notes    : Model routing by query complexity is correct. Gap: no discussion of how to
                     determine complexity reliably at routing time without being expensive itself.
                     A lightweight complexity classifier is needed — not another LLM call. Also
                     missing: per-course budget caps to prevent runaway costs from a misconfigured
                     ingestion job or a single heavy user.

[09:50:57] user
embedding model upgrade wen we want to move to a better model — how do we do it without downtime

[09:51:07] assistant
Blue-green reindex. Keep existing index live and serving. Build new index in a shadow table in
          parallel — model_id column on embeddings makes this possible. Once shadow index is fully
          built: run retrieval eval suite against both. If new index wins by threshold, update a
          config flag pointing retrieval to the new table. Keep old table 48h for rollback. Zero-
          downtime. The model_id column is load-bearing for this operation.

[09:51:39] user
token cost at scale. 10k daily active users 5 queries each. how do we think about this

[09:51:50] assistant
50K queries/day. Embedding: ~$0.10/day (negligible). Generation with routing: 70% factual -> Haiku,
          30% conceptual -> Sonnet. Average context 800 input + 200 generation tokens. Haiku: 35K x
          1K = ~$8.75. Sonnet: 15K x 1K = ~$45. Total: ~$54/day, ~$1,620/month. With 30% cache hit
          rate: ~$1,130/month. Every query correctly routed to Haiku instead of Sonnet saves 10x.
          Monitor cost-per-query by type and tune routing thresholds.

[09:52:15] user
reranking at scale — cross encoders add latency. lighter option

[09:52:24] assistant
ColBERT-style late interaction. Encodes query and document separately at token level — each query
          token interacts with each document token via MaxSim. 10x faster than full cross-encoders,
          approaches their accuracy. Can self-host. Cohere Rerank API with aggressive batching
          (top-20 not top-50) also keeps added latency under 100ms. Pragmatic question first: if
          retrieval precision@5 is already above 0.80, what specific failure mode are you solving?
          Measure, then add.


──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
  08 — Monitoring & Observability
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

[09:52:59] user
instrumentation. wat is non-negotiable from day one. not nice to have — the things where if we dont
          have them we're flying blind

[09:53:12] assistant
Non-negotiable from day one:

(1) end-to-end query latency — p50/p95/p99 broken down by stage (classify, embed, retrieve,
          generate)
without stage breakdown you cannot diagnose where degradation originates
(2) retrieval hit rate — % of queries returning at least one chunk above similarity threshold 0.7
low hit rate = index problems, not a model problem
(3) faithfulness score distribution — not just average
bimodal (many 3s AND many 1s) is more dangerous than a uniform 2.0
(4) error rate by type — extraction failures, embedding API errors, generation timeouts
(5) cache hit rate per layer — tells you if caching is actually working

[09:53:40] user
user satisfaction signal. they wont rate every response. how do we get implicit signal

[09:53:50] assistant
Implicit signals are more reliable than explicit ratings. Track: follow-up question (user asks
          another = engaged), copy action (user copies text = found it useful enough to keep),
          lecture resumption within 60 seconds of asking (confusion resolved), repeat question on
          same concept within 24h (didn't understand the first answer), session length trend. Build
          a composite engagement score from these. More honest than a 5-star rating 80% of users
          skip.

[09:54:20] user
alerting. wat pages someone at 2am vs wat waits for morning

[09:54:31] assistant
2am (human action required right now):
- query error rate > 10% for 5 minutes — service is broken for users
- LLM API completely unavailable with no cache covering it — students getting nothing
- ingestion queue backed up > 500 jobs for 30+ minutes — content failing at scale

morning (important, not emergency):
- faithfulness daily average drops > 0.3 from 7-day baseline — quality regression
- retrieval hit rate below 0.6 — index health issue
- daily cost exceeds budget threshold — financial alert

principle: page someone only when human action right now improves user experience

[09:55:06] user
feedback loop. how does production data make the system better over time. i dont want to build this
          and forget it

[09:55:18] assistant
Flywheel by design. Week 1–4: collect real query logs, retrieval results, responses, implicit
          engagement signals. Week 4: analyze — which queries have low retrieval hit rates? Which
          cause session abandonment? Which repeat within a session (stuck students)? This becomes
          your first real eval set grounded in actual usage. Month 2: use low-satisfaction pairs to
          identify specific failures, fix them. Month 3: with enough volume, fine-tune the query
          classifier on your actual distribution. The system gets better because you defined the
          feedback loop from day one — not as an afterthought.

[09:55:56] user
go/no-go criteria for production. wat does ready to ship actually mean for this system

[09:56:10] assistant
Checklist:
retrieval     precision@5 > 0.75 on golden eval set
generation    faithfulness > 2.5, correctness > 2.5 on judge eval
latency       p95 first token < 1.5s at 50 concurrent users
security      multi-tenant isolation verified (not assumed), prompt injection suite passing
reliability   graceful degradation tested at each failure layer — API down, retrieval down, both
          down
observability all non-negotiable metrics live, at least one alert configured and tested
rollout       staged plan ready — 5% -> 20% -> 100% with observation period at each gate
rollback      defined trigger: if abandonment rate increases > 15% vs pre-RAG baseline within
48h of launch, rollback immediately

not on the checklist: perfect answers
ship when it's good enough, fast enough, and safe enough — then measure and improve

